[Comment 4045997144 | 2026-03-12T11:28:33Z | https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-ibm-runtime/issues/2649#issuecomment-4045997144]
I was unable to reproduce this issue.

In the composer, I created the following circuit:
```
OPENQASM 2.0;
include "qelib1.inc";

qreg q[2];
creg c[4];
h q[0];
cx q[0], q[1];
barrier q;
cx q[0], q[1];
barrier q[0], q[1];
cx q[0], q[1];
barrier q[0], q[1];
cx q[0], q[1];
barrier q;
cx q[0], q[1];
barrier q[0], q[1];
cx q[0], q[1];
barrier q[0], q[1];
cx q[0], q[1];
measure q[0] -> c[0];
measure q[1] -> c[1];
```

Then I execute it in `ibm_torino`. The job page says that the following circuit is running:
```
OPENQASM 2.0;
include "qelib1.inc";
qreg q[133];
creg c[4];
rz(pi/2) q[0];
sx q[0];
rz(pi/2) q[0];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
cz q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
barrier q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
cz q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
barrier q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
cz q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
barrier q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
cz q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
barrier q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
cz q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
barrier q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
cz q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
barrier q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
cz q[0],q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
sx q[1];
rz(pi/2) q[1];
measure q[0] -> c[0];
measure q[1] -> c[1];
```

Notice the barriers are still there.

If you are trying to do ZNE, consider using the Qiskit SDK instead of the composer. You will have much more control on the qubit allocation.

Closing as `cant repro`.

[Comment 4053067063 | 2026-03-13T06:27:46Z | https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-ibm-runtime/issues/2649#issuecomment-4053067063]
Thank you for attempting to reproduce my experiment and for your detailed
response.

However, I would like to respectfully point out that showing a transpiled
circuit structure is not equivalent to reproducing experimental results. A
complete reproduction requires actual measurement data.

My original submission included:

1) Real hardware execution on ibm_torino.
2) Verifiable Job IDs for every circuit.
3) Actual fidelity measurements across 6 circuit configurations.

Your response shows the transpiled QASM circuit, but does not include:

1) Actual fidelity/measurement results.
2) Job IDs from your execution.
3) Any statistical data from running the circuit.

The core of my finding is not just about circuit structure — it is about
the measured fidelity behavior. Fidelity increasing from 85.2% (1x CX) to
91.2% (11x CX) is the anomaly, regardless of what the transpiled circuit
looks like.
Could you please share the actual measurement results and corresponding job
IDs from your run on ibm_torino?

That would allow a proper comparison.

I remain open to discussion if the data supports a different conclusion.



